    <span class="section-label">The honest answer</span>
    <h1>What happens <em>if I disappear?</em></h1>
    <p>The hardest question a serious buyer asks a sole-proprietor agency is some version of <em>what if you get hit by a bus?</em> Most agencies dodge it. This page is the answer in writing, and it covers the legal, technical, and human posture that makes the relationship survive me being unavailable.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:980px">

    <div class="prose">

      <p style="font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:2.5rem;padding:1.5rem 1.75rem;background:var(--bg-alt);border-left:3px solid var(--gold-500);border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0">
        <strong>The short version:</strong> the code is yours, the hosting is yours, the domain is yours, and a named backup developer has read access to every client repo. If I am unavailable for more than 30 days, the runbook is published in this document and the next person can keep your site running without losing a beat.
      </p>

      <h2>1. The code is yours.</h2>
      <p>Every client site lives in a private GitHub repository. The <strong>monthly plan</strong> contractually grants you the right to receive the full source on thirty days' notice if the agency cannot continue operations. The <strong>lump-sum plan</strong> already transfers source ownership on launch, with the repo sitting in your GitHub organization from day one.</p>
      <p>Either way, you own the deliverable. There is no "platform code" living only on my machine, and the git history goes with you when you go.</p>

      <h2>2. The hosting is yours.</h2>
      <p>Sites are deployed to <a href="https://pages.cloudflare.com" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">Cloudflare Pages</a>. The Pages project lives in <em>your</em> Cloudflare account rather than mine, with me added only as a collaborator who has deploy permissions. If I disappear, you remove my collaborator access and the site keeps serving from your account, on your domain, indefinitely.</p>
      <p>The same posture applies to DNS, namely your nameservers, your records, and my involvement reduced to read-only documentation. You can hire a different developer to deploy to the same Cloudflare project tomorrow without changing a single setting.</p>

      <h2>3. Your domain is yours.</h2>
      <p>I never register domains on a client's behalf. You buy the domain at your registrar of choice (Cloudflare, Namecheap, Porkbun, etc.); I configure the DNS records; the registrar relationship stays with you. If I vanish, the domain does not.</p>

      <h2>4. The named backup developer.</h2>
      <p>A named, vetted independent developer is on retainer with read access to the agency's GitHub organization and a documented runbook. The arrangement is real rather than hypothetical. If I am unreachable for thirty days, they have authorization to do the following:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Push emergency fixes to any client site.</li>
        <li>Respond to client emails from the agency address with a clear "Jon is unavailable; here's what's happening" note.</li>
        <li>Process the 30-day code-handoff for any client who requests it.</li>
        <li>Refund any pre-paid balance.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Their name and contact appear here once the arrangement is finalized. Until then, the legal continuity in section 7 covers the gap.</p>

      <h2>5. The public runbook.</h2>
      <p>The agency runs from a documented internal playbook covering every phase of a client engagement, including first contact, discovery, proposal, onboarding, design, build, pre-launch QA, launch, maintenance, emergencies, billing, and offboarding. The playbook is the operating manual, and if I vanish, the next person can run the agency from those documented steps.</p>
      <p>The playbook lives in the same private repository as the site code, alongside the <a href="/owners-guide/" class="inline-link">owner's guide</a> that serves as your client-facing manual. On hand-off, both ship together.</p>

      <h2>6. The 30-day grace window.</h2>
      <p>If I am unreachable for more than thirty days, whether the cause is an emergency, an illness, or anything else, a client may invoke the continuity clause in the service agreement. The agency is then obligated to provide the following:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Full source code transfer to the client's nominated GitHub account.</li>
        <li>Cloudflare Pages project ownership transfer (already in client account on most engagements; explicit transfer otherwise).</li>
        <li>Refund of any unearned monthly fees, prorated to the day.</li>
        <li>A 60-minute introduction call between the client and a recommended replacement developer.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The clause is in the public <a href="/agreement/" class="inline-link">service agreement</a> and is contractual, not aspirational.</p>

      <h2>7. Legal continuity.</h2>
      <p>The agency operates as a sole-proprietorship (or LLC, depending on jurisdiction) with a named successor on file with the registered agent. In the event of permanent incapacity, the successor is authorized to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Execute the 30-day grace window above for every active client.</li>
        <li>Wind down active engagements with full code handoff and prorated refunds.</li>
        <li>Issue final 1099s and tax documents.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This is paperwork-level continuity, not business continuity. The successor is not expected to keep building websites. The successor is expected to honor existing commitments and hand off cleanly. That is the contract.</p>

      <h2>8. What this is not.</h2>
      <p>This page is not a promise that the agency will run forever. It is the opposite, namely an explicit acknowledgment that a sole-proprietor agency has a real continuity question, paired with a documented posture that addresses the question in writing. The buyer who reads this page should leave with two things: <strong>(a)</strong> confidence that their site, code, hosting, and domain are independent of any single human's availability, and <strong>(b)</strong> realistic expectations about the kind of relationship they are entering into.</p>

      <h2>9. The honest read.</h2>
      <p>If your business genuinely cannot tolerate even a thirty-day pause in development work, a sole-proprietor agency is structurally the wrong shape for you, and you should hire a five-or-more-person agency with redundant developers on staff. The Service Site Standard is for service-business owners who value <em>continuity of authorship</em> more than headcount redundancy, who understand that the trade-off for "one owner, end to end" is real, and who decide that the trade-off is worth it for their particular situation.</p>
      <p>If you are unsure, the discovery call is the right venue to talk it through. I will not try to talk anyone into the wrong shape of relationship.</p>

